## Introduction
**Genetic engineering** has enabled the creation of **genetically modified (GM) foods**, sparking one of the most polarised debates in science — balancing food security against safety and ethical concerns.

## What genetic engineering is
- The **laboratory alteration of an organism's DNA** (adding, deleting or editing genes) to produce desired traits — also called genetic modification.
- **Applications**: agriculture (pest-resistant **Bt cotton**, herbicide-tolerant crops, biofortified **Golden Rice**), medicine (insulin, vaccines, gene therapy) and industry (enzymes).

## The debate on GM food
- **Arguments in favour**:
  - **Food security and higher yields**: pest/disease/drought/salinity resistance for reliable output;
  - **Reduced pesticide use** (Bt crops make their own insecticide);
  - **Enhanced nutrition** (biofortification — Golden Rice's vitamin A);
  - Reduced post-harvest losses and farmer costs.
- **Arguments against**:
  - **Human-health concerns** (allergenicity, long-term safety uncertainty);
  - **Environmental risks** (gene flow to wild relatives, harm to non-target organisms, resistance development, biodiversity loss);
  - **Socio-economic concerns** (corporate control of seeds, farmer dependence, IPR);
  - **Ethical and labelling** issues ('right to know').

## The Indian position
- **Bt cotton** (2002) is the only commercial GM crop; **Bt brinjal** is under moratorium; **GM mustard** is cleared but litigated — reflecting the precaution-versus-productivity tension, regulated by **GEAC**.

## Conclusion
GM foods offer real potential for **food security and nutrition** but raise legitimate **health, environmental and socio-economic** concerns. A **case-by-case, science-based, transparent and well-regulated** approach — with rigorous biosafety testing and labelling — is the balanced path between innovation and precaution.

