## Introduction
**Sterilisation** is fundamental to microbiology, medicine and biotechnology, while **transgenic animals** represent an advanced application of genetic engineering with wide benefits.

## (a) Sterilisation methods
- **Physical methods**:
  - **Moist heat (autoclaving)**: steam under pressure (**121°C, 15 psi**) — for surgical instruments and culture media;
  - **Dry heat** (hot-air oven, 160–170°C) — for glassware, powders, oils;
  - **Filtration** (0.22 µm membranes) — for **heat-sensitive fluids** (vaccines, antibiotics);
  - **Irradiation** — **UV** (air/surfaces) and **gamma rays** (disposable medical equipment, food);
  - **Ultrasonic waves** for delicate solutions.
- **Chemical methods**:
  - **Gaseous** (ethylene oxide, formaldehyde) — for heat-sensitive plastics and catheters;
  - **Liquid chemicals** (alcohols, glutaraldehyde, hypochlorite) — for surfaces and instruments.
- **Choice depends on the material and purpose** (heat-stable vs sensitive).

## (b) Transgenic animals and benefits
- **Transgenic animals**: animals whose genome has been **deliberately modified by inserting a foreign gene**.
- **Benefits**:
  - **Medical ('pharming')**: producing therapeutic proteins in milk (e.g., clotting factors);
  - **Disease models** for research (cancer, Alzheimer's);
  - **Improved traits** (growth, disease resistance, milk/wool yield);
  - **Xenotransplantation** (organs for humans);
  - Study of **gene function**.

## Conclusion
Sterilisation methods — physical (heat, filtration, irradiation) and chemical — ensure microbe-free materials essential to health and biotech, while **transgenic animals** advance medicine, research and agriculture. Both are core tools of modern biotechnology, to be applied with appropriate **safety and ethical** care.
